ild the pot while having equity.\n- **All-in (-2)**: Overkill here; too early to commit all chips without knowing river.\n\nBest move: **Semi-bluff raise** to 300 or so — puts pressure, builds pot, and has high expected value given your outs.\n\nBut note: you acted first on the flop and turn, and opponent showed aggression on the turn. Still, your hand is too strong to fold, and checking again might invite another bet you’ll have to call anyway.\n\nRaising shows strength and leverages your draw.\n\nReason:  \nI have a **strong draw (9 outs to flush)** and **a pair of 7s**, giving me significant equity (~35–40% chance to improve to flush or two pair/trips). Raising acts as a **semi-bluff**, potentially making a weaker hand fold (like middle pair or ace high). Even if called, I’m in good shape to win on the river. A moderate raise (300) balances aggression and chip preservation.\n\nAnswer: 300"}
